WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:06.970
&gt;&gt; Good morning everybody.

00:00:06.971 --> 00:00:09.100
It is my great pleasure to

00:00:09.101 --> 00:00:12.370
welcome Joanna Bryson to
Microsoft Research today,

00:00:12.371 --> 00:00:14.200
for her to give her talk,

00:00:14.201 --> 00:00:15.940
and she's going to be
spending the day here

00:00:15.941 --> 00:00:18.755
talking to a bunch of us.

00:00:18.756 --> 00:00:23.405
She is an Associate Professor
at the University of Perth,

00:00:23.406 --> 00:00:27.054
and she is a trusted
disciplinary researcher

00:00:27.055 --> 00:00:29.780
focusing on many
different problems,

00:00:29.781 --> 00:00:32.320
many problems that are
very interesting to us

00:00:32.321 --> 00:00:35.380
at the intersection
of AI psychology,

00:00:35.381 --> 00:00:38.035
autonomy, ethics of robotics

00:00:38.036 --> 00:00:41.060
and human cooperation
collaboration.

00:00:41.061 --> 00:00:43.330
So, I'll welcome her to talk

00:00:43.331 --> 00:00:45.730
about her fascinating research,

00:00:45.731 --> 00:00:47.250
and if you have questions during

00:00:47.251 --> 00:00:50.560
the talk please free
to ask. Thank you.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.755
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you. I'm
not using that microphone.

00:00:57.756 --> 00:01:00.790
Okay. Thanks for having me here.

00:01:00.791 --> 00:01:02.790
I have as usual a lot of slides.

00:01:02.791 --> 00:01:05.405
So, I'll just get into
it, please do ask.

00:01:05.406 --> 00:01:08.690
But thanks very much, everybody
involved inviting me.

00:01:08.691 --> 00:01:11.630
Originally Jamie, but it's
become a very team effort.

00:01:11.631 --> 00:01:14.590
So, yeah you may

00:01:14.591 --> 00:01:15.700
think it's weird that I'm talking

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:17.345
about the European Union.

00:01:17.346 --> 00:01:18.580
But I'm just taking it as

00:01:18.581 --> 00:01:20.830
another interesting
political entity,

00:01:20.831 --> 00:01:22.960
and they are these very
specific goals which

00:01:22.961 --> 00:01:25.475
are human dignity
and flourishing.

00:01:25.476 --> 00:01:27.035
I don't know about you,

00:01:27.036 --> 00:01:29.200
when I first heard about
these goals it sounded

00:01:29.201 --> 00:01:32.045
like you know like you
know vaporware right.

00:01:32.046 --> 00:01:33.455
Like what does that even mean.

00:01:33.456 --> 00:01:38.410
Right? But I've come to
realize that sort of

00:01:38.411 --> 00:01:40.330
what it means is
that everybody needs

00:01:40.331 --> 00:01:41.860
at least enough stability in

00:01:41.861 --> 00:01:43.480
their lives so that
they can plan,

00:01:43.481 --> 00:01:45.110
so that they can
build a business.

00:01:45.111 --> 00:01:46.870
So, they can decide
whether to have a family.

00:01:46.871 --> 00:01:48.850
Not that everybody needs
a family but that you can

00:01:48.851 --> 00:01:51.460
have these kinds of
things as options, right?

00:01:51.461 --> 00:01:54.215
So, there's something that we
need in order to flourish,

00:01:54.216 --> 00:01:56.695
and we've been doing a pretty
good job of flourishing.

00:01:56.696 --> 00:02:00.285
Do I get a laser? I
could just point.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:04.145
So, this is the log
scale population

00:02:04.146 --> 00:02:05.725
in millions of humans.

00:02:05.726 --> 00:02:08.345
It started from 10,000 years ago.

00:02:08.346 --> 00:02:11.040
Does anyone have a laser that
they're carrying around?

00:02:11.041 --> 00:02:12.530
Oh, here it is. I found one.

00:02:12.531 --> 00:02:14.130
So, yeah.

00:02:14.131 --> 00:02:16.390
So, what happened
10,000 years ago,

00:02:16.391 --> 00:02:17.950
up until 10,000 years ago,

00:02:17.951 --> 00:02:19.900
we were being
out-competed by monkeys.

00:02:19.901 --> 00:02:21.770
Monkeys evolved more
recently than humans.

00:02:21.771 --> 00:02:24.110
There were more macaques
than hominids,

00:02:24.111 --> 00:02:26.300
and then all of a
sudden, we got writing,

00:02:26.301 --> 00:02:28.935
agriculture urbanization and
then dark channel religion

00:02:28.936 --> 00:02:30.250
as an AI person I'd like to

00:02:30.251 --> 00:02:32.650
think the writing
is the key thing.

00:02:32.651 --> 00:02:35.420
But I like to talk
about writing as

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:37.480
the first AI because it
was was off-board memory.

00:02:37.481 --> 00:02:38.965
It allowed us to innovate

00:02:38.966 --> 00:02:41.070
and then all of a
sudden we get this.

00:02:41.071 --> 00:02:43.145
Amazing, so we're flourishing.

00:02:43.146 --> 00:02:45.710
Right. As we know,

00:02:45.711 --> 00:02:47.520
sometimes you might
also think about this,

00:02:47.521 --> 00:02:50.620
an exponential, that's
super-intelligence, right?

00:02:50.621 --> 00:02:52.220
As we've all heard,

00:02:52.221 --> 00:02:58.160
my fellow immigrant
colleague in the UK,

00:02:58.161 --> 00:03:00.975
there is sometimes
unanticipated sub-goals

00:03:00.976 --> 00:03:02.380
of super-intelligence,

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:03.980
and one is the we're wiping out

00:03:03.981 --> 00:03:05.850
of all the other large animals.

00:03:05.851 --> 00:03:09.035
You can see that even before
that 100,00 years ago,

00:03:09.036 --> 00:03:12.510
when humans got to continent
it was sometimes bad news,

00:03:12.511 --> 00:03:16.050
but actually, some of that is

00:03:16.051 --> 00:03:18.360
because there was actually

00:03:18.361 --> 00:03:20.160
another bit of
global warming right then.

00:03:20.161 --> 00:03:22.800
So, maybe the reason we
were moving so much was

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:24.450
because there was
reasons to go and

00:03:24.451 --> 00:03:26.295
that was hard. But look at this.

00:03:26.296 --> 00:03:28.785
There's actually more biomass now

00:03:28.786 --> 00:03:32.580
especially since
the industrial revolution we're

00:03:32.581 --> 00:03:35.940
actually taking energy out
of the ground and out of

00:03:35.941 --> 00:03:39.300
the sky and things and
creating more biomass,

00:03:39.301 --> 00:03:41.250
but we're converting it

00:03:41.251 --> 00:03:44.625
entirely into people
and livestock.

00:03:44.626 --> 00:03:49.425
So, this is another
unanticipated sub-goal.

00:03:49.426 --> 00:03:51.440
It's the green boxes there.

00:03:51.441 --> 00:03:53.135
This is from a few years ago.

00:03:53.136 --> 00:03:58.235
Are all the wild animals there
left, wild mammals, okay.

00:03:58.236 --> 00:04:01.275
Of course, another
unanticipated sub-goal is

00:04:01.276 --> 00:04:03.860
that we have started helping

00:04:03.861 --> 00:04:06.800
produce even more climate
change and that kind of

00:04:06.801 --> 00:04:08.180
thing brings all kinds
of things we

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:10.160
don't really necessarily want.

00:04:10.161 --> 00:04:12.975
All right. I've got to say it,

00:04:12.976 --> 00:04:15.540
all that is not only
because of climate,

00:04:15.541 --> 00:04:18.950
there's lots of things
that drive that change.

00:04:18.951 --> 00:04:21.525
One of the things is the economy.

00:04:21.526 --> 00:04:23.220
So, sometimes we
have these periods

00:04:23.221 --> 00:04:24.940
with very high inequality,

00:04:24.941 --> 00:04:27.065
and that tends to correlate with

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:30.395
very high political polarization
and identity politics.

00:04:30.396 --> 00:04:32.950
So, periods of
very great volatility.

00:04:32.951 --> 00:04:34.905
So, this is World War One,

00:04:34.906 --> 00:04:37.310
does anyone want to guess
why we have numbers going

00:04:37.311 --> 00:04:40.300
back to World War One?

00:04:40.301 --> 00:04:43.400
There was this crazy thing
where we outlawed alcohol,

00:04:43.401 --> 00:04:46.270
and when we outlawed alcohol,

00:04:46.271 --> 00:04:49.185
we needed more tax so
we create income tax.

00:04:49.186 --> 00:04:51.990
So, since the innovation
of the income tax,

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:53.400
we know what proportion of

00:04:53.401 --> 00:04:55.360
wealth has gone to
the top one percent,

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.600
and we can see that
that correlates pretty

00:04:57.601 --> 00:05:00.580
well with the amount of
political polarization.

00:05:00.581 --> 00:05:02.040
This is measured by

00:05:02.041 --> 00:05:05.205
whether or not the two
houses of Congress,

00:05:05.206 --> 00:05:07.290
for both houses of Congress,

00:05:07.291 --> 00:05:10.440
the two parties, both their
names are on bills.

00:05:10.441 --> 00:05:12.780
Okay, so that's
a particular measure due to

00:05:12.781 --> 00:05:15.050
Nolan McCarty and
actually his PhD supervisor

00:05:15.051 --> 00:05:17.290
as he's now professor
at Princeton.

00:05:17.291 --> 00:05:19.845
Anyway, these are
pretty well correlated

00:05:19.846 --> 00:05:22.145
and there's some interesting
things going on there,

00:05:22.146 --> 00:05:23.820
but we, in the 30s,

00:05:23.821 --> 00:05:25.865
in both the US and the UK,

00:05:25.866 --> 00:05:27.735
we managed to get
our act together

00:05:27.736 --> 00:05:32.305
and reduce both inequality
and the polarization,

00:05:32.306 --> 00:05:35.310
and we kept it down
until about 1978

00:05:35.311 --> 00:05:36.785
and then we've
started launching up

00:05:36.786 --> 00:05:39.295
again on both of those measures.

00:05:39.296 --> 00:05:41.625
I think this is history.

00:05:41.626 --> 00:05:43.200
But I think that part
of the reason there's

00:05:43.201 --> 00:05:44.910
more of a gap here than there,

00:05:44.911 --> 00:05:46.050
is because this is measured

00:05:46.051 --> 00:05:48.570
income inequality but probably

00:05:48.571 --> 00:05:51.190
wealth inequality is
what does the drive-in.

00:05:51.191 --> 00:05:54.020
All right. So, we don't have

00:05:54.021 --> 00:05:57.155
income for a longer
time but we do have

00:05:57.156 --> 00:06:00.410
polarization since the Civil War

00:06:00.411 --> 00:06:02.660
and more broadly we can look

00:06:02.661 --> 00:06:05.060
at people believe
they have inequality.

00:06:05.061 --> 00:06:07.050
I don't know if I
believe the Y axis

00:06:07.051 --> 00:06:09.490
here and that you can really
match it up this way.

00:06:09.491 --> 00:06:11.450
Famously, there are some papers

00:06:11.451 --> 00:06:14.060
here Scheidel claiming
that you have to have

00:06:14.061 --> 00:06:16.040
war and mayhem or else you don't

00:06:16.041 --> 00:06:18.235
get reductions of inequality.

00:06:18.236 --> 00:06:20.140
But I think that things
like this matters,

00:06:20.141 --> 00:06:21.875
see that shift in the- ?

00:06:21.876 --> 00:06:25.130
This was between wars
when we did that.

00:06:25.131 --> 00:06:28.175
So, I think we can do
things with policy to

00:06:28.176 --> 00:06:31.810
reduce inequality and
polarization as well.

00:06:31.811 --> 00:06:33.740
So, yeah, why do we care?

00:06:33.741 --> 00:06:34.830
One reason we care and this

00:06:34.831 --> 00:06:36.140
has just come out
in the last year,

00:06:36.141 --> 00:06:37.205
it's been a big question.

00:06:37.206 --> 00:06:38.480
We know there's a correlation.

00:06:38.481 --> 00:06:41.420
What the causation is
has been a big question.

00:06:41.421 --> 00:06:44.480
One of the things we know is
that extreme physicians and

00:06:44.481 --> 00:06:45.590
politicians are driven by

00:06:45.591 --> 00:06:47.840
extreme positions in
major single donors.

00:06:47.841 --> 00:06:50.510
So, one of the things that
inequality does is increase

00:06:50.511 --> 00:06:51.920
this number of people who can

00:06:51.921 --> 00:06:53.760
be such major single donors.

00:06:53.761 --> 00:06:55.820
Right? Because there's
relatively few people

00:06:55.821 --> 00:06:56.990
with large amounts of

00:06:56.991 --> 00:06:58.865
money and this has been changing

00:06:58.866 --> 00:07:01.790
spectacularly in
the last couple of decades.

00:07:01.791 --> 00:07:04.580
All right. So, you've probably
wondered by this point,

00:07:04.581 --> 00:07:07.845
"Is she going to talk
about AI or not?" Right?

00:07:07.846 --> 00:07:11.880
So, mostly I've been talking
about right now, so far,

00:07:11.881 --> 00:07:14.140
is setting the groundwork

00:07:14.141 --> 00:07:16.490
with the impediment to
a stable society part.

00:07:16.491 --> 00:07:18.140
Now, I'm not going
to talk about AI,

00:07:18.141 --> 00:07:20.345
I'm just going to talk
about intelligence.

00:07:20.346 --> 00:07:22.790
So, I realized this

00:07:22.791 --> 00:07:24.690
is Microsoft Research,
I don't mean to pander,

00:07:24.691 --> 00:07:25.970
but this is a slide
I already had and

00:07:25.971 --> 00:07:27.290
I was lazy, but also,

00:07:27.291 --> 00:07:28.700
I think it's
interesting to see that

00:07:28.701 --> 00:07:30.375
you can pitch it this way.

00:07:30.376 --> 00:07:32.760
So, I like to remind people that

00:07:32.761 --> 00:07:36.290
intelligence is relying on
computation and not math.

00:07:36.291 --> 00:07:38.490
A lot of people just assume

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:40.900
that intelligence
can be spontaneous,

00:07:40.901 --> 00:07:42.930
it's absolutely true that AI is

00:07:42.931 --> 00:07:45.750
necessarily going to be
unbiased and neutral,

00:07:45.751 --> 00:07:48.410
and they're mistaking
algorithms for math,

00:07:48.411 --> 00:07:50.080
and math for computation.

00:07:50.081 --> 00:07:52.540
So, computation is
a physical process,

00:07:52.541 --> 00:07:54.505
it's a transformation
of information.

00:07:54.506 --> 00:07:58.315
Any physical process takes
time, energy, and space.

00:07:58.316 --> 00:08:00.935
We all know this is sort
of the Patrick Winston

00:08:00.936 --> 00:08:02.600
about what not that
there's anything

00:08:02.601 --> 00:08:04.310
wrong with that version of AI,

00:08:04.311 --> 00:08:06.640
but finding the right thing
to do at the right time,

00:08:06.641 --> 00:08:08.985
which is the fundamental problem
of intelligence,

00:08:08.986 --> 00:08:10.580
is a form of search.

00:08:10.581 --> 00:08:12.230
The cause search is the number

00:08:12.231 --> 00:08:14.475
of options versus number of acts.

00:08:14.476 --> 00:08:17.740
So, that's why, again,
you guys know this,

00:08:17.741 --> 00:08:18.920
that means that if
you have a robot

00:08:18.921 --> 00:08:20.235
that can do 100 things,

00:08:20.236 --> 00:08:21.660
and then it gets a two step plan,

00:08:21.661 --> 00:08:23.500
there's 10,000 possible
plans already.

00:08:23.501 --> 00:08:25.870
But believe me, you give

00:08:25.871 --> 00:08:27.560
this slide to a politicians even,

00:08:27.561 --> 00:08:30.830
people who are pretty well
informed and they're stunned.

00:08:30.831 --> 00:08:34.010
The whole thing about 35 moves
against the chest,

00:08:34.011 --> 00:08:35.795
not moving the rock
back and forth.

00:08:35.796 --> 00:08:37.805
People don't understand this.

00:08:37.806 --> 00:08:40.350
So, you guys, being at Microsoft,

00:08:40.351 --> 00:08:41.150
are probably thinking "Hasn't

00:08:41.151 --> 00:08:42.815
she heard about the Cloud yet?"

00:08:42.816 --> 00:08:47.320
The concurrency can
save on real time,

00:08:47.321 --> 00:08:51.070
but it obviously,
you have the time,

00:08:51.071 --> 00:08:53.600
you double the amount of
space you require and that

00:08:53.601 --> 00:08:56.425
energy requirements
are a constant.

00:08:56.426 --> 00:08:57.830
Weirdly, people aren't even

00:08:57.831 --> 00:09:00.285
talking about what's
going on with quantum.

00:09:00.286 --> 00:09:02.200
Again, it's going to
be this magic thing,

00:09:02.201 --> 00:09:03.255
it's going to solve our stuff.

00:09:03.256 --> 00:09:05.370
It does seem to save on space.

00:09:05.371 --> 00:09:07.200
Once, for some subset
of problems,

00:09:07.201 --> 00:09:09.875
you can solve things
in a single computer.

00:09:09.876 --> 00:09:12.805
But so far, it looks
like the energy is more

00:09:12.806 --> 00:09:16.355
than the space loss.

00:09:16.356 --> 00:09:18.300
Although there's not enough
good work done on this.

00:09:18.301 --> 00:09:21.070
So, this is my personal
captor quantum physicist,

00:09:21.071 --> 00:09:22.890
but I've given
this talk to a lot of

00:09:22.891 --> 00:09:25.525
places and nobody has told
me I'm wrong about this.

00:09:25.526 --> 00:09:27.570
But basically, nobody is doing

00:09:27.571 --> 00:09:31.180
math on the energy
problems of quantum.

00:09:31.181 --> 00:09:33.390
So, yes. Omniscience is not

00:09:33.391 --> 00:09:36.880
actually a real threat.
That's not the problem.

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:39.870
Again, I probably don't need
to tell anyone in this room,

00:09:39.871 --> 00:09:43.470
but I have been in DC where
people were talking about,

00:09:43.471 --> 00:09:44.760
"What if one country finds

00:09:44.761 --> 00:09:46.070
the machine learning algorithm,

00:09:46.071 --> 00:09:48.530
and suddenly knows
all the things."

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.340
Then, that somehow there'd be

00:09:51.341 --> 00:09:53.360
no way to recover from that.

00:09:53.361 --> 00:09:55.860
National Intelligence
is not general.

00:09:55.861 --> 00:09:57.490
Again, I wrote the slide

00:09:57.491 --> 00:09:59.420
talk yesterday and nobody
said this to me, this might.

00:09:59.421 --> 00:10:00.800
Nobody has so far and

00:10:00.801 --> 00:10:03.100
the three people I've talked
to has mentioned of AGI.

00:10:03.101 --> 00:10:06.160
But anyway, I'll mention it.

00:10:06.161 --> 00:10:07.790
Early in psychology, we thought

00:10:07.791 --> 00:10:10.040
that any stimulus could
provoke any response.

00:10:10.041 --> 00:10:11.870
We were wrong.

00:10:11.871 --> 00:10:14.665
It turns out pigeons can
learn to peck for food,

00:10:14.666 --> 00:10:17.020
and deflect their wings
to avoid shock.

00:10:17.021 --> 00:10:19.785
Incidentally, not
every pigeon can learn this.

00:10:19.786 --> 00:10:21.425
Most pigeons don't
peck that much.

00:10:21.426 --> 00:10:23.100
That's actually the genius
pigeons who also that

00:10:23.101 --> 00:10:24.780
were used for all those
experiments you've seen.

00:10:24.781 --> 00:10:26.345
People tell you, "Oh, pigeons."

00:10:26.346 --> 00:10:29.765
But anyway, pigeons can
learn to peck for food,

00:10:29.766 --> 00:10:31.720
they can not learn
to peck in order to

00:10:31.721 --> 00:10:34.255
avoid shock or to flap
their wings for food.

00:10:34.256 --> 00:10:36.100
That doesn't make sense
if you're a pigeon.

00:10:36.101 --> 00:10:37.210
Those kinds of things are stuff

00:10:37.211 --> 00:10:38.220
you would never need to know.

00:10:38.221 --> 00:10:39.720
So, if those activity
is not there,

00:10:39.721 --> 00:10:40.890
your brain doesn't provide you

00:10:40.891 --> 00:10:43.585
the bias to do
that kind of learning.

00:10:43.586 --> 00:10:46.080
So, yeah.

00:10:46.081 --> 00:10:47.850
This is the advantage of

00:10:47.851 --> 00:10:50.195
science over some other
sources of knowledge.

00:10:50.196 --> 00:10:53.430
Okay. Now, that we
understand combinatorics,

00:10:53.431 --> 00:10:54.590
this is one of the big questions

00:10:54.591 --> 00:10:56.040
I had as an undergraduate.

00:10:56.041 --> 00:10:58.540
Why is it that the brain,

00:10:58.541 --> 00:11:00.430
given that there's
evolution and everything,

00:11:00.431 --> 00:11:03.410
why hasn't the brain settled
down on one architecture?

00:11:03.411 --> 00:11:05.250
Why haven't we figured
out the best way

00:11:05.251 --> 00:11:06.350
to represent information?

00:11:06.351 --> 00:11:07.540
Why is there different activity

00:11:07.541 --> 00:11:08.735
in different parts of the brain?

00:11:08.736 --> 00:11:09.920
Well, different parts
of the brain

00:11:09.921 --> 00:11:11.575
are solving different problems.

00:11:11.576 --> 00:11:12.890
So, there's going to be different

00:11:12.891 --> 00:11:14.825
optimal solutions to
these different problems.

00:11:14.826 --> 00:11:16.600
So, of course, you're going
to get different activity

00:11:16.601 --> 00:11:18.300
in different
representations and things.

00:11:18.301 --> 00:11:20.280
Nobody really, when you
think about a heart,

00:11:20.281 --> 00:11:22.090
expects that the whole brain

00:11:22.091 --> 00:11:23.975
is going like one giant retina.

00:11:23.976 --> 00:11:26.970
Come on, it's just
doing different stuff.

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:33.659
So, that also explains
why some level of bias,

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:37.290
even prejudice, is
a necessary part of AI.

00:11:37.291 --> 00:11:39.560
Again, I think you guys

00:11:39.561 --> 00:11:41.590
know about this work,
but I don't know.

00:11:41.591 --> 00:11:44.510
How many of you people know
about my science paper,

00:11:44.511 --> 00:11:48.085
the one about that word
embeddings have prejudice?

00:11:48.086 --> 00:11:49.900
Okay. How many people know about

00:11:49.901 --> 00:11:51.810
your guy's paper that
came out that, well,

00:11:51.811 --> 00:11:56.145
it hit our archives at
the same time but went NIPS?

00:11:56.146 --> 00:11:58.805
Okay. So, there was,

00:11:58.806 --> 00:12:01.100
for those of you don't know, that

00:12:01.101 --> 00:12:03.080
was entirely
an overlapping group.

00:12:03.081 --> 00:12:04.930
But within everyone. All right.

00:12:04.931 --> 00:12:06.440
So, I will do this a little bit.

00:12:06.441 --> 00:12:09.835
So, if you just use
word embeddings,

00:12:09.836 --> 00:12:11.310
which is basically
a giant vector.

00:12:11.311 --> 00:12:12.555
I'm going to show
you a picture later.

00:12:12.556 --> 00:12:13.830
So, you basically just sit around

00:12:13.831 --> 00:12:16.270
and count whether words,

00:12:16.271 --> 00:12:18.365
or how words co-occur
next to each other,

00:12:18.366 --> 00:12:20.060
you recover the things that

00:12:20.061 --> 00:12:22.330
scientists call implicit biases.

00:12:22.331 --> 00:12:25.670
So, we took every single
study we could find.

00:12:25.671 --> 00:12:27.925
This is because
the first author was

00:12:27.926 --> 00:12:29.080
a Princeton postdoc and

00:12:29.081 --> 00:12:30.200
the final author was up

00:12:30.201 --> 00:12:31.860
for tenure this year
at Princeton.

00:12:31.861 --> 00:12:34.460
Every single paper that had

00:12:34.461 --> 00:12:38.730
the implicit association
test in it and used words,

00:12:38.731 --> 00:12:40.960
and used them on
the general population.

00:12:40.961 --> 00:12:42.420
Because we were using the Web,

00:12:42.421 --> 00:12:43.670
and we couldn't tell who

00:12:43.671 --> 00:12:44.750
had written what part of the Web,

00:12:44.751 --> 00:12:47.005
so, we could only use
general population results.

00:12:47.006 --> 00:12:49.355
But we matched
every single one of them.

00:12:49.356 --> 00:12:51.495
So, this for example, is showing,

00:12:51.496 --> 00:12:53.970
you have a bunch of women's
names representing women,

00:12:53.971 --> 00:12:55.925
and men's names representing men,

00:12:55.926 --> 00:12:58.665
and family words representing
the domestic sphere,

00:12:58.666 --> 00:13:01.990
and career words representing
the corporate sphere.

00:13:01.991 --> 00:13:06.700
Then you find, if the world
was a perfectly fair place,

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:10.440
if you give them the task
of matching words up,

00:13:10.441 --> 00:13:12.930
they should be just as fast at

00:13:12.931 --> 00:13:16.510
matching up women to
career and men to family,

00:13:16.511 --> 00:13:17.750
as the other way around,

00:13:17.751 --> 00:13:21.805
but in fact they're much faster
at doing women to career,

00:13:21.806 --> 00:13:23.870
to family, and men to career.

00:13:23.871 --> 00:13:27.640
So, this is them being
faster like regular people,

00:13:27.641 --> 00:13:30.645
and d is a measurement in
terms of standard deviation.

00:13:30.646 --> 00:13:32.590
Because we don't have a theory
of how long that should

00:13:32.591 --> 00:13:35.480
take but we just look and say,

00:13:35.481 --> 00:13:37.030
"Are these two things different?"

00:13:37.031 --> 00:13:38.720
So, we see the normal
distribution, we say,

00:13:38.721 --> 00:13:40.995
"How far apart are those
two normal distributions?"

00:13:40.996 --> 00:13:43.045
These are giants. This is huge.

00:13:43.046 --> 00:13:44.730
When people say, "Oh,

00:13:44.731 --> 00:13:46.100
this is the real effect."

00:13:46.101 --> 00:13:47.810
What are they talking about?

00:13:47.811 --> 00:13:50.070
This is one of the biggest
effects in psychology,

00:13:50.071 --> 00:13:51.450
implicit association test,

00:13:51.451 --> 00:13:53.845
and people are say
it's fake news.

00:13:53.846 --> 00:13:56.020
Because, it doesn't correlate

00:13:56.021 --> 00:13:58.010
perfectly with
explicit prejudice.

00:13:58.011 --> 00:13:59.620
I think that's because
people don't know

00:13:59.621 --> 00:14:01.070
the difference between implicit,

00:14:01.071 --> 00:14:02.680
and explicit memory
anymore which we

00:14:02.681 --> 00:14:05.400
got tired if you're old
as I am in college.

00:14:05.401 --> 00:14:10.490
Anyway, so the point is
that they were saying this,

00:14:10.491 --> 00:14:11.830
and I still get people who hammer

00:14:11.831 --> 00:14:13.000
me when I give that talk.

00:14:13.001 --> 00:14:15.405
I didn't think I needed
to give that talk here,

00:14:15.406 --> 00:14:18.560
saying, "Don't you know that
you can't trust psychology?"

00:14:18.561 --> 00:14:21.380
Totally different method,
doing word counting,

00:14:21.381 --> 00:14:23.510
large purpose linguistics,
the same stuff, latent,

00:14:23.511 --> 00:14:25.540
and semantic analysis,
the way that we do

00:14:25.541 --> 00:14:29.265
search for web pages.

00:14:29.266 --> 00:14:31.035
We were able to replicate,

00:14:31.036 --> 00:14:32.635
well, replicate is not
quite the right word.

00:14:32.636 --> 00:14:36.040
Also find evidence of
bias in the words.

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:38.160
The headlines all said AI is

00:14:38.161 --> 00:14:39.920
prejudice and
the good headline said,

00:14:39.921 --> 00:14:41.465
"It is because of us."

00:14:41.466 --> 00:14:43.050
But that wasn't
really the finding.

00:14:43.051 --> 00:14:46.080
The real finding was
that words or word use,

00:14:46.081 --> 00:14:48.285
what we pass on to other people,

00:14:48.286 --> 00:14:52.195
as well as to machines,
contains the image,

00:14:52.196 --> 00:14:56.170
not only of our implicit
biases, but actually,

00:14:56.171 --> 00:14:57.250
and this is something completely

00:14:57.251 --> 00:14:58.540
down to that final author,

00:14:58.541 --> 00:15:00.580
I mentioned Divya
Narayanan, he said,

00:15:00.581 --> 00:15:03.255
"I want to see what happens
with something that's real."

00:15:03.256 --> 00:15:05.920
So, the Y axis here is

00:15:05.921 --> 00:15:08.690
the same awful prejudice word
embeddings that

00:15:08.691 --> 00:15:10.890
gave us that women are closer to

00:15:10.891 --> 00:15:14.020
the domestic sphere than
to the work sphere.

00:15:14.021 --> 00:15:17.530
So, those exact same
things, but then,

00:15:17.531 --> 00:15:21.165
we went out and looked at
the 2015 US labor statistics,

00:15:21.166 --> 00:15:23.465
which unfortunately,
weren't all single terms,

00:15:23.466 --> 00:15:24.920
and this only works
for single term.

00:15:24.921 --> 00:15:26.550
So, we had to just throw
away the adjectives.

00:15:26.551 --> 00:15:28.230
So, that's why there's
some of the noise,

00:15:28.231 --> 00:15:31.120
but, 90 percent correlation.

00:15:31.121 --> 00:15:33.705
Again, if you're in psychology,
that's a huge deal.

00:15:33.706 --> 00:15:36.495
Ninety percent correlation,

00:15:36.496 --> 00:15:40.100
in predicting how male
or female a word was,

00:15:40.101 --> 00:15:41.885
this is a programmer
unfortunately.

00:15:41.886 --> 00:15:43.080
When I was a programmer,

00:15:43.081 --> 00:15:45.120
we were 13 percent
of the population,

00:15:45.121 --> 00:15:47.460
women were, and this is the '80s.

00:15:47.461 --> 00:15:48.635
It went up it and peaked around

00:15:48.636 --> 00:15:50.930
18-19 percent, it's crashed.

00:15:50.931 --> 00:15:53.210
It's four or five percent,
it's just appalling.

00:15:53.211 --> 00:15:54.430
I don't know why and

00:15:54.431 --> 00:15:55.895
there's a lots of
women in the audience.

00:15:55.896 --> 00:15:58.315
Programming is fun.
I love programming.

00:15:58.316 --> 00:15:59.490
I don't know what's happened.

00:15:59.491 --> 00:16:02.185
Anyway. So, yeah.

00:16:02.186 --> 00:16:05.430
This is like being a nurse
or something up here.

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:08.440
Fortunately, being a doctor,
is over here somewhere.

00:16:08.441 --> 00:16:11.685
But anyway, I learned
a lot last summer.

00:16:11.686 --> 00:16:15.570
But the point is, that

00:16:15.571 --> 00:16:17.730
our implicit biases turn out to

00:16:17.731 --> 00:16:19.695
reflect the current reality

00:16:19.696 --> 00:16:22.230
in some cases, not all of them.

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:26.190
That's interesting. It's cool.

00:16:26.191 --> 00:16:29.135
Because going back to the
implicit, explicit divide,

00:16:29.136 --> 00:16:32.115
that means that we can
explicitly decide,

00:16:32.116 --> 00:16:33.815
"Oh, we want to employ women.

00:16:33.816 --> 00:16:36.800
We think it's good for women
to be able to have jobs,

00:16:36.801 --> 00:16:39.090
and we can try to shift
to something new."

00:16:39.091 --> 00:16:41.180
But the implicit biases seem to

00:16:41.181 --> 00:16:43.440
be bootstrapping off
of observed reality,

00:16:43.441 --> 00:16:45.500
or historic reality,
or something.

00:16:45.501 --> 00:16:50.170
So, go ahead and ask
me more questions.

00:16:50.171 --> 00:16:51.230
But anyway, that wasn't going

00:16:51.231 --> 00:16:52.285
to be the point at this talk.

00:16:52.286 --> 00:16:55.995
Sorry. So, the point is that,

00:16:55.996 --> 00:16:59.650
when you're a member of
a cognitive species, then,

00:16:59.651 --> 00:17:01.424
it makes sense for you to exploit

00:17:01.425 --> 00:17:03.965
the concurrent computation
that others are doing.

00:17:03.966 --> 00:17:05.440
Again, I didn't have time,

00:17:05.441 --> 00:17:06.960
I put way too many
slides into this,

00:17:06.961 --> 00:17:08.520
but, I have a beautiful video,

00:17:08.521 --> 00:17:10.230
ask me if you want it in Q&amp;A,

00:17:10.231 --> 00:17:12.440
of tortoises learning
from each other,

00:17:12.441 --> 00:17:14.330
and they're not very smart.

00:17:14.331 --> 00:17:16.165
These tortoises are not smart,

00:17:16.166 --> 00:17:17.670
but when they see
a tortoise solve

00:17:17.671 --> 00:17:19.495
a problem, they solve
the problem too.

00:17:19.496 --> 00:17:22.475
It's a problem they
couldn't solve for a month.

00:17:22.476 --> 00:17:25.100
So, it just makes sense.

00:17:25.101 --> 00:17:27.460
This is the way to think
about what culture is;

00:17:27.461 --> 00:17:30.850
it's the exploitation of
computations already been done.

00:17:30.851 --> 00:17:32.670
So, we can do this

00:17:32.671 --> 00:17:34.810
explicitly with students
or apprentices,

00:17:34.811 --> 00:17:37.260
I just mentioned you can have
implicit social learning,

00:17:37.261 --> 00:17:38.450
even if they're
really stupid things

00:17:38.451 --> 00:17:40.435
like reptiles and fish.

00:17:40.436 --> 00:17:42.560
Sorry I didn't put
a reference in there,

00:17:42.561 --> 00:17:45.040
just ask me for
the references if you want.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:48.250
One of the things that's
going on is we've

00:17:48.251 --> 00:17:50.679
implicit sensitivity
to social examples,

00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:52.375
and I think that's
what's going on with

00:17:52.376 --> 00:17:56.090
the overactive
anthropomorphism of AI, okay?

00:17:56.091 --> 00:17:57.880
So, now, I'll come down
and start actually

00:17:57.881 --> 00:18:00.185
talking about artificial
intelligence, all right?

00:18:00.186 --> 00:18:02.645
I think, yeah. Not quite.

00:18:02.646 --> 00:18:05.015
Almost. Just before AI.

00:18:05.016 --> 00:18:06.955
I was asked to give a keynote

00:18:06.956 --> 00:18:10.365
at RoboPhil which you'll
probably have heard of it,

00:18:10.366 --> 00:18:13.085
but it's actually a fairly
big meeting of philosophers

00:18:13.086 --> 00:18:15.960
and people working
on social robots.

00:18:15.961 --> 00:18:17.940
So, things that are supposed
to look like people and

00:18:17.941 --> 00:18:20.555
participate in
human society, okay?

00:18:20.556 --> 00:18:23.380
This is the fourth or
fifth time that it's met,

00:18:23.381 --> 00:18:24.790
I think it is a biannual.

00:18:24.791 --> 00:18:26.180
But it's the first
time they met in

00:18:26.181 --> 00:18:27.660
Vienna and it's the first time

00:18:27.661 --> 00:18:30.305
they invited more general
scientists to it.

00:18:30.306 --> 00:18:31.875
They titled it this year,

00:18:31.876 --> 00:18:34.410
Politics, Power, and
the Public Space.

00:18:34.411 --> 00:18:36.335
Okay, speaking of public spaces,

00:18:36.336 --> 00:18:39.195
this is a statue
by Seward Johnson.

00:18:39.196 --> 00:18:42.210
He produced a whole lot
of statues in

00:18:42.211 --> 00:18:45.110
the '70s and put them
into public spaces.

00:18:45.111 --> 00:18:47.105
In the '70s, that
was going a little

00:18:47.106 --> 00:18:49.030
dicey in some of
these public spaces.

00:18:49.031 --> 00:18:51.630
It turned out that if
you put something looked

00:18:51.631 --> 00:18:54.345
like a person into
a public space,

00:18:54.346 --> 00:18:56.675
you've made that space safer.

00:18:56.676 --> 00:18:59.030
People felt more like there

00:18:59.031 --> 00:19:01.125
was another person
around, all right?

00:19:01.126 --> 00:19:04.160
That's the statue, okay?

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:08.790
A lot of philosophers had
this huge thing about

00:19:08.791 --> 00:19:12.470
intuition like you can tell
that a robot is a person,

00:19:12.471 --> 00:19:13.720
you can tell that.

00:19:13.721 --> 00:19:16.350
How could you be
denying something,

00:19:16.351 --> 00:19:18.680
denying rights to
something that you can

00:19:18.681 --> 00:19:20.760
feel is definitely a person?

00:19:20.761 --> 00:19:23.650
You feel the statue is
a person too, okay?

00:19:23.651 --> 00:19:25.410
You can show that you
feel that because it

00:19:25.411 --> 00:19:27.860
alters your behavior, all right?

00:19:27.861 --> 00:19:35.250
So, over scaled statues
fascinate yet diminish us.

00:19:35.251 --> 00:19:36.920
That's something
Seward Johnson actually said.

00:19:36.921 --> 00:19:39.390
So, he got bored of making
human scale once he started

00:19:39.391 --> 00:19:42.130
taking pieces of art
and employing them up.

00:19:42.131 --> 00:19:43.785
It's really cool.
Those are my parents

00:19:43.786 --> 00:19:46.045
hanging out at the statue.

00:19:46.046 --> 00:19:49.065
This a place in New Jersey
called Grounds for Sculpture.

00:19:49.066 --> 00:19:52.125
So, yeah.

00:19:52.126 --> 00:19:54.660
There's a concept from
psychology, again,

00:19:54.661 --> 00:19:58.880
just being this, super
normal stimulus,

00:19:58.881 --> 00:20:02.430
that if you have something
that you evolved to attend

00:20:02.431 --> 00:20:06.170
to and that you never go into
an evolutionary history,

00:20:06.171 --> 00:20:08.800
if it's something that was
a greater example of that,

00:20:08.801 --> 00:20:10.760
you can actually over
respond to it and

00:20:10.761 --> 00:20:13.515
your biology doesn't shut
you down from doing that.

00:20:13.516 --> 00:20:15.365
Arguably, we've been doing things

00:20:15.366 --> 00:20:17.475
like this for a long time.

00:20:17.476 --> 00:20:23.850
So, what happens with AI?

00:20:23.851 --> 00:20:25.500
This is how I got into AI ethics.

00:20:25.501 --> 00:20:27.130
So, I have a couple
degrees in psychology

00:20:27.131 --> 00:20:28.965
but I also a couple
degrees in AI.

00:20:28.966 --> 00:20:30.705
When I started my PhD,

00:20:30.706 --> 00:20:32.385
I was on this project,

00:20:32.386 --> 00:20:34.845
this robot, at the time
that I was on it and

00:20:34.846 --> 00:20:37.235
at the time this picture
was taken, did not work.

00:20:37.236 --> 00:20:41.165
This is basically a sculpture
made out of motors, okay?

00:20:41.166 --> 00:20:42.815
But it turns out there was

00:20:42.816 --> 00:20:44.520
all these processors
that were supposed

00:20:44.521 --> 00:20:46.360
to be at sprain

00:20:46.361 --> 00:20:48.595
and it turned out they
weren't properly grounded.

00:20:48.596 --> 00:20:50.660
So, anyway, the
whole time I was on

00:20:50.661 --> 00:20:53.250
the project, it didn't work.

00:20:53.251 --> 00:20:57.415
Yet people would walk by and say,

00:20:57.416 --> 00:20:59.585
"It would be unethical
to unplug that."

00:20:59.586 --> 00:21:01.410
They were proud to know

00:21:01.411 --> 00:21:03.020
that they had this
certain knowledge that,

00:21:03.021 --> 00:21:06.170
I couldn't unplug
this thing, all right?

00:21:06.171 --> 00:21:09.500
They generalize from feminism

00:21:09.501 --> 00:21:10.820
and the civil rights movement.

00:21:10.821 --> 00:21:13.075
They found out that
the most unlikeliest things

00:21:13.076 --> 00:21:15.245
turn out to be people, right?

00:21:15.246 --> 00:21:16.615
That they could just
say, "Oh, yeah.

00:21:16.616 --> 00:21:18.990
Well, obviously, if
a woman could be a person,

00:21:18.991 --> 00:21:21.180
then this is too, right?"

00:21:21.181 --> 00:21:24.660
So, anyway. Yeah.
There's something

00:21:24.661 --> 00:21:25.720
really strange going on here.

00:21:25.721 --> 00:21:27.460
They really, really get angry if

00:21:27.461 --> 00:21:29.685
you say that this
is not a person,

00:21:29.686 --> 00:21:31.645
and that they say
that we have a right

00:21:31.646 --> 00:21:32.870
to fall in love
with these things,

00:21:32.871 --> 00:21:36.320
we have a right to have our
best friends that we can buy;

00:21:36.321 --> 00:21:38.655
that we can buy and
sell citizens, right?

00:21:38.656 --> 00:21:41.740
So, I think this is both
sick and dangerous.

00:21:41.741 --> 00:21:43.605
I say that as somebody who

00:21:43.606 --> 00:21:45.150
seriously would thought
she was going to

00:21:45.151 --> 00:21:46.430
dedicate an entire PhD to

00:21:46.431 --> 00:21:48.240
making this into
a child, all right?

00:21:48.241 --> 00:21:50.375
So, I realize, I get
it, I've been there.

00:21:50.376 --> 00:21:53.815
But, you can read some
of the papers about this

00:21:53.816 --> 00:21:55.410
Kathleen Richardson
particular talks

00:21:55.411 --> 00:21:58.060
about this in
the feminist context.

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:00.400
So, this is me back then.

00:22:00.401 --> 00:22:04.250
If you're young like
that, now be aware.

00:22:04.251 --> 00:22:07.570
But that robot on the left,

00:22:07.571 --> 00:22:08.880
the robot on the right didn't

00:22:08.881 --> 00:22:10.570
work at the time, it
did eventually work.

00:22:10.571 --> 00:22:12.305
The one on the left
already worked,

00:22:12.306 --> 00:22:14.670
nobody ever said like, "Oh,

00:22:14.671 --> 00:22:17.055
to be honest, I can unplug
the insect robots."

00:22:17.056 --> 00:22:18.225
Even though they actually worked,

00:22:18.226 --> 00:22:20.670
they were smarter in
some sense, right?

00:22:20.671 --> 00:22:22.815
So, yeah, and I've
got an unplug code.

00:22:22.816 --> 00:22:24.490
My supervisor at that time,

00:22:24.491 --> 00:22:25.995
again, I didn't finish with him.

00:22:25.996 --> 00:22:27.700
I call a conservative for saying

00:22:27.701 --> 00:22:29.390
robots might not take over.

00:22:29.391 --> 00:22:31.155
People were absolutely certain

00:22:31.156 --> 00:22:33.495
that robots were going
to take over, right?

00:22:33.496 --> 00:22:35.160
This is now, again,
random people.

00:22:35.161 --> 00:22:37.420
This was people on
a panel he was on of

00:22:37.421 --> 00:22:41.495
other scientists and
philosophers and AI builders.

00:22:41.496 --> 00:22:44.275
Okay. So, because of all this,

00:22:44.276 --> 00:22:46.625
I wrote with the only
philosopher I knew at the time.

00:22:46.626 --> 00:22:48.010
We wrote this in 1996.

00:22:48.011 --> 00:22:49.105
It took us forever to get it into

00:22:49.106 --> 00:22:50.710
the crappiest possible workshop.

00:22:50.711 --> 00:22:53.980
Then, finally, we did a rewrite

00:22:53.981 --> 00:22:57.930
and reboot and put it
into IJCAI more recently.

00:22:57.931 --> 00:23:00.660
But this is 1998 text.

00:23:00.661 --> 00:23:03.030
"We believe exaggerated
fears of and hopes

00:23:03.031 --> 00:23:05.070
for AI are symptomatic
of a larger problem,

00:23:05.071 --> 00:23:06.690
a general confusion
about the nature of

00:23:06.691 --> 00:23:09.145
humanity and the role
of ethics in society.

00:23:09.146 --> 00:23:11.915
Our thesis is that
these exaggerated fears are

00:23:11.916 --> 00:23:13.790
false concerns which
can distract us from

00:23:13.791 --> 00:23:16.080
the real dangers of
AI technologies."

00:23:16.081 --> 00:23:18.850
Often, I get cast as
somebody who is saying,

00:23:18.851 --> 00:23:20.530
"Oh, there's nothing to
worry about with AI."

00:23:20.531 --> 00:23:22.150
I don't know, you're worrying
about the wrong things

00:23:22.151 --> 00:23:23.760
with AI, right?

00:23:23.761 --> 00:23:25.820
"The real dangers are
no different though

00:23:25.821 --> 00:23:29.430
from those of other artifacts
so potential for misuse,

00:23:29.431 --> 00:23:30.830
either through carelessness or

00:23:30.831 --> 00:23:33.525
malevolence by the people
who control them."

00:23:33.526 --> 00:23:35.410
All right. So this is

00:23:35.411 --> 00:23:38.640
a slide that I wrote when
I started giving talks

00:23:38.641 --> 00:23:39.980
because of the things
people were saying

00:23:39.981 --> 00:23:46.555
about our AI biased paper
that came out, right?

00:23:46.556 --> 00:23:48.995
So, a lot of people
said, "Oh, look,

00:23:48.996 --> 00:23:52.570
this is terrible
because society is bad,

00:23:52.571 --> 00:23:54.040
therefore AI is going to be bad."

00:23:54.041 --> 00:23:58.230
So, yes, you can have
biases in this case.

00:23:58.231 --> 00:24:01.045
I don't like to use
biases but anyway.

00:24:01.046 --> 00:24:03.375
They absorbed automatically by

00:24:03.376 --> 00:24:04.920
a machine learning
from ordinary culture.

00:24:04.921 --> 00:24:06.600
That's true, that's
one source of them.

00:24:06.601 --> 00:24:09.120
There's also
accidental bias that's

00:24:09.121 --> 00:24:11.760
being introduced
through ignorance by,

00:24:11.761 --> 00:24:13.205
for example, the Kate Crawford

00:24:13.206 --> 00:24:15.125
insufficiently diverse
development team.

00:24:15.126 --> 00:24:16.440
Yes, that's also a problem.

00:24:16.441 --> 00:24:18.630
But the main thing I
worry about is that

00:24:18.631 --> 00:24:20.800
she could deliberately
introduce bias.

00:24:20.801 --> 00:24:22.410
Somebody could say, "Hey,

00:24:22.411 --> 00:24:24.050
I've been elected only
by the people from

00:24:24.051 --> 00:24:27.540
the East Coast or
the east side of town,

00:24:27.541 --> 00:24:29.450
let's figure out a way to save

00:24:29.451 --> 00:24:31.380
money and let's just
take it on the west side

00:24:31.381 --> 00:24:32.920
because they weren't
voting for me anyway so we

00:24:32.921 --> 00:24:34.905
don't need to worry
about that," right?

00:24:34.906 --> 00:24:36.585
You can come and do that.

00:24:36.586 --> 00:24:40.020
If you forget that AI is
something somebody built,

00:24:40.021 --> 00:24:41.765
then nobody is going to guess,

00:24:41.766 --> 00:24:44.050
nobody is going to notice, right?

00:24:44.051 --> 00:24:45.565
Nobody was talking about this,

00:24:45.566 --> 00:24:46.585
they were all sitting there in

00:24:46.586 --> 00:24:48.945
the New York Times talking about

00:24:48.946 --> 00:24:52.130
a Silicon Valley's
problem with lack of

00:24:52.131 --> 00:24:55.970
diversity which is an issue
but it's not the issue.

00:24:55.971 --> 00:24:59.595
It's an issue. Right. Yeah, by

00:24:59.596 --> 00:25:01.225
the way, back to
what I was saying.

00:25:01.226 --> 00:25:04.720
Yeah, this is the most bypass
I had by magnitude

00:25:04.721 --> 00:25:06.085
ever and

00:25:06.086 --> 00:25:09.055
the malevolence AI book,
It's come out since then.

00:25:09.056 --> 00:25:10.710
But, yeah, malevolence, right?

00:25:10.711 --> 00:25:13.980
Malevolence, deliberate,
right. So, anyway,

00:25:13.981 --> 00:25:16.320
ethical instincts
and ethics itself

00:25:16.321 --> 00:25:18.615
we claimed was rooted
in identity, right?

00:25:18.616 --> 00:25:20.795
That's something
a lot of people said.

00:25:20.796 --> 00:25:24.055
We, humans mistakenly place

00:25:24.056 --> 00:25:25.605
language mathematics
and reason as

00:25:25.606 --> 00:25:27.280
the core of humanity because it

00:25:27.281 --> 00:25:28.440
discriminates us from animals

00:25:28.441 --> 00:25:30.150
and then we can use
them in stuff, right?

00:25:30.151 --> 00:25:33.620
We thought back in 1996 as
a civil who wrote this,

00:25:33.621 --> 00:25:35.275
that once we have
empirical experience of

00:25:35.276 --> 00:25:37.700
AI this confusion is
probably going to go away.

00:25:37.701 --> 00:25:39.910
We're talking about
like, how can we get

00:25:39.911 --> 00:25:42.480
children the experience
of building AI,

00:25:42.481 --> 00:25:44.900
young so they can be
smarter about this?

00:25:44.901 --> 00:25:46.580
Right. Then, maybe even we'd

00:25:46.581 --> 00:25:48.980
start informing our human ethics.

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:52.545
So, the sound is not coming on.

00:25:52.546 --> 00:25:59.560
So, this is just

00:25:59.561 --> 00:26:01.775
an undergraduate funding a course

00:26:01.776 --> 00:26:04.035
on Intelligent Control
and Cognitive Systems.

00:26:04.036 --> 00:26:08.260
But they didn't develop
these robots to be social,

00:26:08.261 --> 00:26:12.290
they had just been doing in
a room but listen to them.

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:16.860
&gt;&gt; It's incredible.

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:27.300
&gt;&gt; I like how it does.

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:30.730
&gt;&gt; I don't know if
you're picking this up,

00:26:30.731 --> 00:26:32.750
to me it's pretty
clear to me that

00:26:32.751 --> 00:26:34.180
these guys are identifying

00:26:34.181 --> 00:26:35.160
with the robots they have built.

00:26:35.161 --> 00:26:36.630
They are projecting
themselves into

00:26:36.631 --> 00:26:38.570
the space and it's as
if they were there,

00:26:38.571 --> 00:26:39.810
that they're falling over.

00:26:39.811 --> 00:26:41.780
They're saying these things,
they're seeing

00:26:41.781 --> 00:26:45.025
themselves in their robots,
they're running around.

00:26:45.026 --> 00:26:46.880
I was talking at
the same to them,

00:26:46.881 --> 00:26:48.780
those robots were built by

00:26:48.781 --> 00:26:50.840
them individually for
their projects and then we had

00:26:50.841 --> 00:26:52.570
this thing called the Beauty
Competition at the end

00:26:52.571 --> 00:26:54.330
of the year to decide
which robots don't have

00:26:54.331 --> 00:26:55.630
to be dismantled but rather can

00:26:55.631 --> 00:26:56.970
be used to attract freshmen to

00:26:56.971 --> 00:27:01.550
University of Bath and to
live for another year.

00:27:01.551 --> 00:27:03.510
The Beauty Competition
space that we bring

00:27:03.511 --> 00:27:06.435
three naive people who don't
know anything about AI.

00:27:06.436 --> 00:27:08.740
One year we used the theory of

00:27:08.741 --> 00:27:11.070
computation people group to

00:27:11.071 --> 00:27:13.800
look at the robots

00:27:13.801 --> 00:27:15.030
and see which one looks most

00:27:15.031 --> 00:27:16.345
intelligent from the outside.

00:27:16.346 --> 00:27:18.495
Okay. So, anyway, the point is

00:27:18.496 --> 00:27:21.410
that the students are seeing

00:27:21.411 --> 00:27:22.460
their robots doing
something they've

00:27:22.461 --> 00:27:23.790
never done before
which is interact with

00:27:23.791 --> 00:27:26.380
other robots and they
project themselves into it.

00:27:26.381 --> 00:27:28.275
We get this final year student at

00:27:28.276 --> 00:27:31.160
a leading computer science
undergraduate to create,

00:27:31.161 --> 00:27:32.290
in Britain, who has

00:27:32.291 --> 00:27:34.260
built and programmed
to run it themselves,

00:27:34.261 --> 00:27:35.975
talking about loving this robots.

00:27:35.976 --> 00:27:39.790
Okay. So, I'm sure

00:27:39.791 --> 00:27:41.960
you can make some kind
of association here.

00:27:41.961 --> 00:27:43.940
I think that at least
for developers,

00:27:43.941 --> 00:27:46.850
that we could have like
the movie actor dissociation,

00:27:46.851 --> 00:27:48.300
but it's that clear to me in

00:27:48.301 --> 00:27:50.460
fact you know anyone who
is an actor knows that

00:27:50.461 --> 00:27:53.890
not everybody can make
the movie character actor

00:27:53.891 --> 00:27:55.330
dissociation anyway.

00:27:55.331 --> 00:27:57.280
Right, it's not clear
what's happening to

00:27:57.281 --> 00:27:59.440
people when we put
robots out there

00:27:59.441 --> 00:28:02.190
that they're able to tell
that there's this narrative

00:28:02.191 --> 00:28:03.440
that we like to play and then

00:28:03.441 --> 00:28:05.135
there's the reality
of the robot. Yes.

00:28:05.136 --> 00:28:07.440
&gt;&gt; I don't think
it's just AI though,

00:28:07.441 --> 00:28:10.140
I've done a study of programmers

00:28:10.141 --> 00:28:13.575
debugging distributed systems
and they say things like,

00:28:13.576 --> 00:28:15.420
"The server says this" and then

00:28:15.421 --> 00:28:17.670
"The client says that", right?

00:28:17.671 --> 00:28:20.520
I don't know if they're
anthropomorphizing it,

00:28:20.521 --> 00:28:23.370
so much as just it's
a useful vocabulary,

00:28:23.371 --> 00:28:25.105
like it's just a useful metaphor

00:28:25.106 --> 00:28:27.015
for describing the technology?

00:28:27.016 --> 00:28:28.220
&gt;&gt; That's certainly true.

00:28:28.221 --> 00:28:31.410
That's actually
a really difficult problem

00:28:31.411 --> 00:28:35.055
especially if you start
becoming known for this stuff.

00:28:35.056 --> 00:28:37.100
You will hear, I have
to go back through

00:28:37.101 --> 00:28:38.410
my proofs and make sure that

00:28:38.411 --> 00:28:39.875
I haven't done that all the time,

00:28:39.876 --> 00:28:41.500
because of course
it was easier to

00:28:41.501 --> 00:28:43.770
say the car once knew
this or whatever.

00:28:43.771 --> 00:28:46.415
So, that is ordinary
language. You're right.

00:28:46.416 --> 00:28:49.110
But, when it becomes something
that an awful lot of

00:28:49.111 --> 00:28:52.800
ordinary culture has told
people really is a person,

00:28:52.801 --> 00:28:54.240
then I think it starts to become

00:28:54.241 --> 00:28:55.780
confusing and so that's why I

00:28:55.781 --> 00:28:59.525
do go back to my proofs and
try to make that change.

00:28:59.526 --> 00:29:01.250
But, yeah.

00:29:01.251 --> 00:29:03.140
I remember when we
used to say like

00:29:03.141 --> 00:29:05.630
multi-agency systems is
going to be a new way to do

00:29:05.631 --> 00:29:08.860
software engineering because
it helps people think better

00:29:08.861 --> 00:29:13.300
about especially in
pro-active context,

00:29:13.301 --> 00:29:14.845
real time pro-active contexts,

00:29:14.846 --> 00:29:19.405
the responsibilities of the
different part of the system.

00:29:19.406 --> 00:29:21.330
If they can use
social reasoning on

00:29:21.331 --> 00:29:22.640
those different aspects and

00:29:22.641 --> 00:29:24.150
there's all this
nice anthropology,

00:29:24.151 --> 00:29:25.960
showing that we're
better at musing about

00:29:25.961 --> 00:29:29.015
humans than about abstractions.

00:29:29.016 --> 00:29:31.650
So anyway, I think this is

00:29:31.651 --> 00:29:34.270
the way I currently
think, what's going on.

00:29:34.271 --> 00:29:36.240
So, I sort of
mentioned this before,

00:29:36.241 --> 00:29:37.640
intelligence is doing
the right thing at

00:29:37.641 --> 00:29:40.180
the right time and
Artificial Intelligence.

00:29:40.181 --> 00:29:41.660
The only difference between

00:29:41.661 --> 00:29:43.490
that and natural is
that it's an artefact,

00:29:43.491 --> 00:29:44.740
it was deliberately made.

00:29:44.741 --> 00:29:47.540
Right. So, agents are
just a vector of change.

00:29:47.541 --> 00:29:48.975
We don't care about agency.

00:29:48.976 --> 00:29:51.595
What we care about
is moral agents.

00:29:51.596 --> 00:29:54.520
Okay? The moral agent
is the thing that

00:29:54.521 --> 00:29:57.580
a society holds responsible
for their actions.

00:29:57.581 --> 00:29:59.050
Now, a lot of people think,

00:29:59.051 --> 00:30:01.570
when you get into ethics that

00:30:01.571 --> 00:30:03.240
there is some absolute ethics

00:30:03.241 --> 00:30:04.520
that we're all
converging towards.

00:30:04.521 --> 00:30:05.820
That somebody has written down

00:30:05.821 --> 00:30:07.510
a set of rules and
carved it in stone,

00:30:07.511 --> 00:30:09.185
and we just need
to figure it out.

00:30:09.186 --> 00:30:11.400
But, I can give you

00:30:11.401 --> 00:30:13.170
very clear examples of

00:30:13.171 --> 00:30:14.920
moral agency being different

00:30:14.921 --> 00:30:16.180
between societies which is that,

00:30:16.181 --> 00:30:19.995
what age is a child responsible
for their own actions.

00:30:19.996 --> 00:30:24.695
Okay? Massive disagreement
about a fundamental question,

00:30:24.696 --> 00:30:28.100
between cultures, even
how old do you need to

00:30:28.101 --> 00:30:29.370
be to say that you want to be

00:30:29.371 --> 00:30:31.175
married or to consent to sex.

00:30:31.176 --> 00:30:33.070
Right? This is something that we

00:30:33.071 --> 00:30:34.895
spend a lot of time
trying to figure out.

00:30:34.896 --> 00:30:36.730
So moral agency is something that

00:30:36.731 --> 00:30:39.445
a society works out for itself.

00:30:39.446 --> 00:30:41.450
While patients see similarly as

00:30:41.451 --> 00:30:43.265
the other side is

00:30:43.266 --> 00:30:45.495
what are the things we
feel responsible for?

00:30:45.496 --> 00:30:48.120
Now, the interesting thing
is that these together

00:30:48.121 --> 00:30:50.770
are moral subjects and if
you look at for example,

00:30:50.771 --> 00:30:53.100
Roman law, they sort of say,

00:30:53.101 --> 00:30:54.880
"You're only a moral or

00:30:54.881 --> 00:30:57.340
a legal patient if
you're a legal agent".

00:30:57.341 --> 00:30:59.130
That the law is about us,

00:30:59.131 --> 00:31:03.180
but that's the opposite of
psychological intuitions,

00:31:03.181 --> 00:31:05.470
so people feel that there's

00:31:05.471 --> 00:31:07.950
more patiency where
there's less agency.

00:31:07.951 --> 00:31:11.710
So, we all are supposed to be
helping babies and a lot of

00:31:11.711 --> 00:31:13.375
people don't feel obliged to

00:31:13.376 --> 00:31:14.670
adults that have addiction

00:31:14.671 --> 00:31:15.830
problems or whatever
they're like,

00:31:15.831 --> 00:31:17.090
"Hey, come on they're
an adult they should take

00:31:17.091 --> 00:31:18.585
care of it themselves."

00:31:18.586 --> 00:31:23.830
So, these are the definitions

00:31:23.831 --> 00:31:25.320
to see if we have
a clear conversation

00:31:25.321 --> 00:31:27.320
about what the
actual problems are.

00:31:27.321 --> 00:31:30.895
Okay. Now this is all just true.

00:31:30.896 --> 00:31:32.765
This is a weird thing
that people hate.

00:31:32.766 --> 00:31:34.580
Okay? So, my claim is that,

00:31:34.581 --> 00:31:36.150
ethics is a set of behaviors

00:31:36.151 --> 00:31:38.390
that creates and
sustains a society,

00:31:38.391 --> 00:31:40.555
including by defining
its identity.

00:31:40.556 --> 00:31:42.080
That's why we get hung up on

00:31:42.081 --> 00:31:43.850
some of the things like okay,

00:31:43.851 --> 00:31:45.545
the British don't eat horses.

00:31:45.546 --> 00:31:47.725
They don't. The people in
Eastern Europe they do.

00:31:47.726 --> 00:31:49.880
But it's a big deal. Yeah.

00:31:49.881 --> 00:31:52.650
&gt;&gt; Probably this is not
your main point but do you

00:31:52.651 --> 00:31:55.540
think visual illusion is
a part of intelligence?

00:31:55.541 --> 00:31:56.950
&gt;&gt; Sorry, visual illusions?

00:31:56.951 --> 00:31:59.635
&gt;&gt; Illusions, yeah.

00:31:59.636 --> 00:32:01.495
&gt;&gt; Well, I think it's
a part of art. Yeah.

00:32:01.496 --> 00:32:04.920
Yes. That's an
interesting question

00:32:04.921 --> 00:32:06.500
that's not directly on
the current narrative,

00:32:06.501 --> 00:32:08.625
so let's save it for
the Q and A. Okay?

00:32:08.626 --> 00:32:09.165
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:32:09.166 --> 00:32:13.650
&gt;&gt; All right. So, ethics

00:32:13.651 --> 00:32:17.130
is something that a society
develops to stabilize itself.

00:32:17.131 --> 00:32:19.420
There are things that will be,

00:32:19.421 --> 00:32:21.580
this includes general principles.

00:32:21.581 --> 00:32:24.180
Right? Like murder and

00:32:24.181 --> 00:32:25.320
theft and things like that

00:32:25.321 --> 00:32:26.870
tend to destabilize the society,

00:32:26.871 --> 00:32:29.000
any society, so societies
will tend to converge

00:32:29.001 --> 00:32:31.635
on those kinds of rules.

00:32:31.636 --> 00:32:34.100
But then as I said, one
of the things that helps

00:32:34.101 --> 00:32:35.300
you keep your society together

00:32:35.301 --> 00:32:36.540
is by maintaining this identity,

00:32:36.541 --> 00:32:38.630
deciding who is a
part of your society.

00:32:38.631 --> 00:32:40.220
That is actually one
of the things by

00:32:40.221 --> 00:32:44.100
which a society defends itself.

00:32:44.101 --> 00:32:45.280
I mean you could
think of it as the

00:32:45.281 --> 00:32:47.535
original part of governance,

00:32:47.536 --> 00:32:51.950
is that the main thing that
governance does is allocate

00:32:51.951 --> 00:32:56.825
resources to defining
an identity for a group,

00:32:56.826 --> 00:32:59.450
to build some kind of defenses

00:32:59.451 --> 00:33:00.645
around a village or something.

00:33:00.646 --> 00:33:02.005
Right? That's that sort of

00:33:02.006 --> 00:33:04.680
redistribution and
security or the sort of

00:33:04.681 --> 00:33:06.560
the central things
that are defining

00:33:06.561 --> 00:33:08.975
a society so it can function
in particular ways.

00:33:08.976 --> 00:33:10.300
&gt;&gt; Is it fair to
say that ethics are

00:33:10.301 --> 00:33:12.530
necessary but not sufficient?

00:33:12.531 --> 00:33:15.050
&gt;&gt; What I'm claiming here,

00:33:15.051 --> 00:33:17.300
is that all the

00:33:17.301 --> 00:33:22.240
behaviors by which we
maintain the society,

00:33:22.241 --> 00:33:23.905
I'm just calling those ethics.

00:33:23.906 --> 00:33:25.795
So, to extent that,

00:33:25.796 --> 00:33:28.410
you need behavior and this is
kind of a weird definition,

00:33:28.411 --> 00:33:29.840
this is an eccentric one for

00:33:29.841 --> 00:33:30.940
communicating now this is not

00:33:30.941 --> 00:33:32.050
what you're going to
find in the dictionary.

00:33:32.051 --> 00:33:36.885
But to the extent that
you need behavior,

00:33:36.886 --> 00:33:40.100
to help the society do
you need other things,

00:33:40.101 --> 00:33:43.950
is that sufficient, while
you need the individuals.

00:33:44.150 --> 00:33:46.585
So, there has to be a society,

00:33:46.586 --> 00:33:48.185
society is made of something.

00:33:48.186 --> 00:33:49.790
&gt;&gt; I will give you very,

00:33:49.791 --> 00:33:50.870
very tough type for example,

00:33:50.871 --> 00:33:52.920
you can have two set
of very diverge of

00:33:52.921 --> 00:33:55.910
those ethics that are collocated.

00:33:55.911 --> 00:33:59.705
Most states, most people
have very different beliefs.

00:33:59.706 --> 00:34:01.870
Is there a society
or two societies?

00:34:01.871 --> 00:34:03.730
&gt;&gt; Right. That's
a really interesting question,

00:34:03.731 --> 00:34:04.640
so as you say.

00:34:04.641 --> 00:34:06.480
So, to some extent there are

00:34:06.481 --> 00:34:08.255
sub societies within
that and then there is

00:34:08.256 --> 00:34:09.470
the super society which

00:34:09.471 --> 00:34:12.545
has presumably some part of
the intersection that in

00:34:12.546 --> 00:34:14.755
some other roles
too including just

00:34:14.756 --> 00:34:18.310
being enforced by
the state powers.

00:34:18.311 --> 00:34:22.925
So, yes. So, okay.

00:34:22.926 --> 00:34:25.270
&gt;&gt; What is the role of
change in that fix?

00:34:25.271 --> 00:34:26.835
Like the temporal change.

00:34:26.836 --> 00:34:28.950
Because this definition
is kind of seeking

00:34:28.951 --> 00:34:31.720
for something more like static.

00:34:31.721 --> 00:34:33.505
&gt;&gt; No. No, it's not.

00:34:33.506 --> 00:34:36.445
Actually, yes, change
is a big issue.

00:34:36.446 --> 00:34:38.260
I don't want to go
into great detail,

00:34:38.261 --> 00:34:39.390
but the whole reason that I was

00:34:39.391 --> 00:34:40.700
setting this definition up,

00:34:40.701 --> 00:34:41.850
which I don't
actually go into that

00:34:41.851 --> 00:34:42.890
much in the rest of the talks,

00:34:42.891 --> 00:34:44.360
so I'll go ahead
and say this here,

00:34:44.361 --> 00:34:47.530
it's because given
that ethics is itself

00:34:47.531 --> 00:34:51.405
an artifact and the AI is
definitionally an artifact,

00:34:51.406 --> 00:34:53.410
there's no fact of
the matter of where we

00:34:53.411 --> 00:34:55.390
stick AI into society.

00:34:55.391 --> 00:34:57.990
That's not a scientifically
discoverable fact.

00:34:57.991 --> 00:35:01.265
It's rather something that
we need to be optimizing.

00:35:01.266 --> 00:35:02.450
So, this comes in to where

00:35:02.451 --> 00:35:03.950
your and my research
is similar that

00:35:03.951 --> 00:35:05.030
we keep doing things like that

00:35:05.031 --> 00:35:06.765
are improving that solution.

00:35:06.766 --> 00:35:08.230
So, the whole reason I'm saying

00:35:08.231 --> 00:35:09.630
this is to get it to people.

00:35:09.631 --> 00:35:11.210
Because while you
think that ethics is

00:35:11.211 --> 00:35:13.110
a static target that
you're moving towards,

00:35:13.111 --> 00:35:14.630
that you're just perfecting,

00:35:14.631 --> 00:35:17.950
believe me you can get
professional philosophers that are

00:35:17.951 --> 00:35:19.240
really angry about the stuff

00:35:19.241 --> 00:35:21.125
that will get up and
say something like,

00:35:21.126 --> 00:35:22.960
"We now know better
than people knew

00:35:22.961 --> 00:35:26.570
in the 1940's or whatever."

00:35:27.010 --> 00:35:29.435
We know some things better.

00:35:29.436 --> 00:35:30.710
I'm not one of those people whose

00:35:30.711 --> 00:35:31.870
a complete cultural relativists.

00:35:31.871 --> 00:35:34.235
I do believe that we are
aggregating more information.

00:35:34.236 --> 00:35:35.675
But I wouldn't say that we have

00:35:35.676 --> 00:35:37.500
the perfect ethics
or that that's even

00:35:37.501 --> 00:35:40.720
definable because it depends
on the particular society.

00:35:40.721 --> 00:35:41.770
The societies have to be

00:35:41.771 --> 00:35:44.460
agile and able to
deal with change.

00:35:44.461 --> 00:35:46.655
So, I would expect it to change.

00:35:46.656 --> 00:35:47.910
So that's not what
I'm going towards.

00:35:47.911 --> 00:35:49.815
In fact, that's what I'm
trying to defend against.

00:35:49.816 --> 00:35:53.630
Okay. We'll come to
some more of that.

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:57.740
Again, this is not
the kinds of things I think

00:35:57.741 --> 00:35:58.840
you guys are concerned about but

00:35:58.841 --> 00:36:00.785
I'll go ahead and
leave the slide in.

00:36:00.786 --> 00:36:09.670
When I say that
AI's place in ethics,

00:36:09.671 --> 00:36:13.085
it doesn't have to be a moral
agent or a moral patient.

00:36:13.086 --> 00:36:17.030
What I'm trying to
say is that we don't

00:36:17.031 --> 00:36:18.690
have to take it as
something we have to

00:36:18.691 --> 00:36:21.200
give that kind of consideration.

00:36:21.201 --> 00:36:23.775
That doesn't mean that we
can't take moral action.

00:36:23.776 --> 00:36:27.090
All right. So, what
is moral actions?

00:36:27.091 --> 00:36:29.920
If you have a behavioral
context that affords

00:36:29.921 --> 00:36:34.450
more than one possible
action and first of all,

00:36:34.451 --> 00:36:37.620
the society believes that some
or one of those actions is

00:36:37.621 --> 00:36:39.270
more socially beneficial and

00:36:39.271 --> 00:36:42.020
the individual is able
to recognize that,

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:48.115
then in that case I would
call that a moral action.

00:36:48.116 --> 00:36:50.295
When you have to
make this decision,

00:36:50.296 --> 00:36:52.100
whether you make a more
or less moral which

00:36:52.101 --> 00:36:54.290
is more or less socially
sanctioned action.

00:36:54.291 --> 00:36:58.355
I've deliberately again set
this up in a way to make it

00:36:58.356 --> 00:36:59.815
totally easy to build

00:36:59.816 --> 00:37:02.470
and you can talk about it
in things like monkeys.

00:37:02.471 --> 00:37:04.570
So, monkeys will punish

00:37:04.571 --> 00:37:06.620
other monkeys that aren't
doing the right thing,

00:37:06.621 --> 00:37:08.250
that don't give food calls,

00:37:08.251 --> 00:37:09.760
or apparently they'll even all

00:37:09.761 --> 00:37:11.975
get a whole troop barking at

00:37:11.976 --> 00:37:14.950
a dominant monkey that's
being too abusive

00:37:14.951 --> 00:37:16.220
of its position of power and

00:37:16.221 --> 00:37:18.300
beating up another
monkey too much.

00:37:18.301 --> 00:37:20.790
So, you can talk about

00:37:20.791 --> 00:37:24.100
that kind of action in
monkeys with a pet.

00:37:24.101 --> 00:37:27.150
If you come home and
the dog's pooed in the middle of

00:37:27.151 --> 00:37:28.780
the rug and you
think the dog knows

00:37:28.781 --> 00:37:31.145
better than this and
you shout at the dog.

00:37:31.146 --> 00:37:32.770
The dog probably thinks you knew

00:37:32.771 --> 00:37:33.805
better than to come home at

00:37:33.806 --> 00:37:36.595
12 at night and that's
why I pooed there.

00:37:36.596 --> 00:37:38.400
So we are both
sanctioning each other.

00:37:38.401 --> 00:37:41.470
But nobody outside of
your household is going to

00:37:41.471 --> 00:37:44.580
consider the dog to be
a moral legal agent or whatever.

00:37:44.581 --> 00:37:47.400
But you're acting within
the society of your family,

00:37:47.401 --> 00:37:48.900
that you thought
the dog knew what

00:37:48.901 --> 00:37:51.465
its options were and that
it chose the wrong one.

00:37:51.466 --> 00:37:56.110
So, I'm not saying the AI
has to be a moral when I say

00:37:56.111 --> 00:37:58.175
that we don't need
to make AI out to

00:37:58.176 --> 00:38:00.725
be moral agents or
moral patients.

00:38:00.726 --> 00:38:05.490
Okay. All right. Yes. So yes.

00:38:05.491 --> 00:38:07.505
Just to be controversial,

00:38:07.506 --> 00:38:09.230
monkeys, children, pets, women,

00:38:09.231 --> 00:38:10.925
slaves and robots can

00:38:10.926 --> 00:38:12.760
autonomously select moral
actions without being

00:38:12.761 --> 00:38:16.385
held to be moral or legal agents
by their wider society.

00:38:16.386 --> 00:38:18.705
I'm not saying I support
all those solutions.

00:38:18.706 --> 00:38:20.670
I'm not saying that I
think it's a good idea

00:38:20.671 --> 00:38:23.150
to consider some people to not be

00:38:23.151 --> 00:38:28.855
responsible or to own
their own actions or property.

00:38:28.856 --> 00:38:30.885
But I'm saying that those
are the kinds of decisions

00:38:30.886 --> 00:38:33.085
societies have previously made.

00:38:33.086 --> 00:38:35.070
I think it's very
important to realize that

00:38:35.071 --> 00:38:37.255
when we talk about human justice,

00:38:37.256 --> 00:38:40.275
it's not about like maintain
some kind of balance,

00:38:40.276 --> 00:38:42.240
a lot of it is about dissuasion.

00:38:42.241 --> 00:38:43.970
So, one of the things
that I'm going

00:38:43.971 --> 00:38:45.510
here with this set of things,

00:38:45.511 --> 00:38:46.760
I'm realizing that
I've put too many

00:38:46.761 --> 00:38:47.810
interesting talks together and

00:38:47.811 --> 00:38:50.455
not drawn quite enough threads,

00:38:50.456 --> 00:38:52.330
but where I'm going with

00:38:52.331 --> 00:38:54.480
this is it doesn't
make sense for the AI

00:38:54.481 --> 00:39:01.885
to be set up as a legal person,

00:39:01.886 --> 00:39:04.710
because we're not going to
be able to sanction it.

00:39:04.711 --> 00:39:06.620
So, we can still have it taking

00:39:06.621 --> 00:39:09.940
legal actions but not
be the legal agent.

00:39:10.260 --> 00:39:13.870
So, monkeys and pets are easily

00:39:13.871 --> 00:39:17.400
dissuadable by things
like social isolation.

00:39:17.401 --> 00:39:20.620
Being isolated is torture
for a social animal,

00:39:20.621 --> 00:39:22.795
like fish that like to school,

00:39:22.796 --> 00:39:24.595
they die of stress
when you take them

00:39:24.596 --> 00:39:26.300
to an isolated situation.

00:39:26.301 --> 00:39:27.930
It is torture for humans.

00:39:27.931 --> 00:39:29.980
So, monkeys and pets are

00:39:29.981 --> 00:39:32.120
dissuaded by the kinds
of things we do.

00:39:32.121 --> 00:39:34.100
They may not care if they
lose their money but they

00:39:34.101 --> 00:39:36.555
care about being isolated
from their families.

00:39:36.556 --> 00:39:39.335
AI and Shell companies,

00:39:39.336 --> 00:39:43.710
you can make it care about
money and things like that,

00:39:43.711 --> 00:39:45.930
but they aren't dissuadable.

00:39:45.931 --> 00:39:47.280
The whole point of
a Shell company

00:39:47.281 --> 00:39:48.470
is that the people who are taking

00:39:48.471 --> 00:39:51.815
the fall are not
the ones who mind.

00:39:51.816 --> 00:39:53.445
That you've set
something up that can

00:39:53.446 --> 00:39:56.235
go bankrupt and nobody
actually cares.

00:39:56.236 --> 00:39:58.580
So, only makes sense
for a company to be

00:39:58.581 --> 00:39:59.960
a legal person if

00:39:59.961 --> 00:40:02.300
someone cares if
that company goes bankrupt.

00:40:02.301 --> 00:40:05.190
I mean, if the people
who are making

00:40:05.191 --> 00:40:06.240
the executive decisions of that

00:40:06.241 --> 00:40:08.715
company care if it goes bankrupt.

00:40:08.716 --> 00:40:11.130
So AI is the ultimate
Shell company,

00:40:11.131 --> 00:40:12.455
and that's what I'm
trying to defend

00:40:12.456 --> 00:40:14.565
against IN this slide.

00:40:14.566 --> 00:40:16.910
If that was a little
too fast and loose,

00:40:16.911 --> 00:40:19.430
we could go into
this really nice formal paper.

00:40:19.431 --> 00:40:21.400
These two guys are
law professors.

00:40:21.401 --> 00:40:22.450
This is just alphabetical.

00:40:22.451 --> 00:40:24.090
He did most of
the good decisions.

00:40:24.091 --> 00:40:26.180
He did most of the good writing.

00:40:26.260 --> 00:40:29.290
But this came out last year.

00:40:29.291 --> 00:40:31.700
So, my conjecture
here is that none of

00:40:31.701 --> 00:40:33.720
things like consciousness,
language, and ethics,

00:40:33.721 --> 00:40:35.735
are the things that mandate
moral subjectivity,

00:40:35.736 --> 00:40:38.070
that we need to worry about AI as

00:40:38.071 --> 00:40:41.510
being a moral agent
or moral patient.

00:40:41.511 --> 00:40:44.135
Oops, I'm going to turn
down the sound on this.

00:40:44.136 --> 00:40:46.120
So yes,

00:40:46.121 --> 00:40:48.500
for billions of years
intelligence, consciousness,

00:40:48.501 --> 00:40:50.260
agency, language,
morality, and divinity

00:40:50.261 --> 00:40:52.330
have all been at least
seen as correlated.

00:40:52.331 --> 00:40:53.880
But as we all know, correlation

00:40:53.881 --> 00:40:55.875
is not equal to causation.

00:40:55.876 --> 00:40:58.755
It drives me crazy when
people sit here and

00:40:58.756 --> 00:41:01.405
worry about when is AI
going to become conscious.

00:41:01.406 --> 00:41:03.020
We can build it to express

00:41:03.021 --> 00:41:06.005
the different definitions
we have of consciousness.

00:41:06.006 --> 00:41:08.865
That doesn't change
its moral status.

00:41:08.866 --> 00:41:11.490
So for example, one of
the things that makes a

00:41:11.491 --> 00:41:13.840
human incredibly valuable is

00:41:13.841 --> 00:41:16.255
the fact that they're
not replaceable.

00:41:16.256 --> 00:41:19.225
Once someone is dead
they're just gone.

00:41:19.226 --> 00:41:21.200
AI you can backup.

00:41:21.201 --> 00:41:22.875
So, even if you had
something and it

00:41:22.876 --> 00:41:24.480
looks exactly like a person,

00:41:24.481 --> 00:41:26.800
if you decided to
have one copy of it

00:41:26.801 --> 00:41:27.960
then you don't have
the same kinds of

00:41:27.961 --> 00:41:30.160
ethical obligations towards it.

00:41:30.360 --> 00:41:32.820
So, yes, a substantial objective.

00:41:32.821 --> 00:41:36.680
So, this is a complain
about philosophers who

00:41:36.681 --> 00:41:40.055
confound these concepts
around a single term.

00:41:40.056 --> 00:41:43.420
I do actually do robots too.

00:41:43.421 --> 00:41:44.985
I'm just talking a lot here.

00:41:44.986 --> 00:41:48.135
It's Aude Billard's robot,
that's my PhD student.

00:41:48.136 --> 00:41:50.010
Anyway, what's interesting to

00:41:50.011 --> 00:41:51.470
me about consciousness is why

00:41:51.471 --> 00:41:52.660
only part of what we do is

00:41:52.661 --> 00:41:54.450
accessible to
explicit description.

00:41:54.451 --> 00:41:56.500
I think we understand that now.

00:41:56.501 --> 00:41:58.530
As I mentioned,
the combinatorics problem,

00:41:58.531 --> 00:42:01.025
you can't be worrying about
all the things all the time.

00:42:01.026 --> 00:42:02.280
But also I think there's a

00:42:02.281 --> 00:42:03.910
philogony that
language evolved from

00:42:03.911 --> 00:42:05.360
the scope of the module
for planning

00:42:05.361 --> 00:42:07.505
action sequences and
things like that,

00:42:07.506 --> 00:42:09.930
and then it became
the means by which we

00:42:09.931 --> 00:42:12.045
negotiate coalition
agreements with others.

00:42:12.046 --> 00:42:14.795
This is why we can
do things like say,

00:42:14.796 --> 00:42:17.520
"Who cares that women
didn't used to have jobs?"

00:42:17.521 --> 00:42:18.680
Now we think it would be more

00:42:18.681 --> 00:42:21.250
fair if women could have jobs.

00:42:21.760 --> 00:42:24.490
The part of us that
used to do planning,

00:42:24.491 --> 00:42:26.530
we also use it for negotiate and

00:42:26.531 --> 00:42:30.070
coalitions and that's part
of creating fairness.

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:33.765
So, that's cool and we could
use AI to do that but again,

00:42:33.766 --> 00:42:36.610
doesn't mean necessarily that

00:42:39.590 --> 00:42:42.250
that has to be a moral agent.

00:42:42.251 --> 00:42:43.450
So, what we experience

00:42:43.451 --> 00:42:45.445
phenomenologically
as consciousness,

00:42:45.446 --> 00:42:47.690
seems to be a point when we

00:42:47.691 --> 00:42:50.495
update important models
and the time that

00:42:50.496 --> 00:42:52.140
we are appropriate to that

00:42:52.141 --> 00:42:56.020
is proportional to how
certain we are about that.

00:42:56.021 --> 00:42:57.380
Again, all of us who work in

00:42:57.381 --> 00:42:59.445
Machine Learning could
imagine doing this if we have

00:42:59.446 --> 00:43:01.515
bottlenecks for
Machine Learning and we

00:43:01.516 --> 00:43:04.030
allocate it to the problems
we're currently working on.

00:43:04.031 --> 00:43:07.080
It's not necessarily
about choosing,

00:43:07.081 --> 00:43:09.280
actually I don't know why
I threw this slide in

00:43:09.281 --> 00:43:11.590
because this is
another great paper.

00:43:11.591 --> 00:43:14.400
I really like it, but
it's a longer talk.

00:43:14.401 --> 00:43:16.515
So, ask me to come back
to that if you want.

00:43:16.516 --> 00:43:19.715
But my point is that
when we are conscious,

00:43:19.716 --> 00:43:21.750
when we're able to
describe what we've done,

00:43:21.751 --> 00:43:23.660
we seem to be in
this particular position

00:43:23.661 --> 00:43:25.885
of updating our current models.

00:43:25.886 --> 00:43:29.020
The same thing, we can do
exactly the same actions and

00:43:29.021 --> 00:43:30.300
not remember having done them

00:43:30.301 --> 00:43:31.845
in a different kind of context.

00:43:31.846 --> 00:43:33.410
So, it's not just about
action selection.

00:43:33.411 --> 00:43:34.605
I think it's about learning.

00:43:34.606 --> 00:43:37.380
But again, I'm trying to
demystify consciousness and say,

00:43:37.381 --> 00:43:38.630
"Look, that's not
the point either."

00:43:38.631 --> 00:43:41.030
The point is moral agency
and moral patience,

00:43:41.031 --> 00:43:43.305
and those are things that
we're collectively defining.

00:43:43.306 --> 00:43:45.070
So we're not just trying
to discover whether

00:43:45.071 --> 00:43:47.785
AI requires that motivation.

00:43:47.786 --> 00:43:49.810
Going back to this,

00:43:49.811 --> 00:43:52.205
remember I mentioned this before,

00:43:52.206 --> 00:43:58.765
all of this stuff this
is what we're doing.

00:43:58.766 --> 00:44:00.545
So, for those of you who
didn't raise your hands,

00:44:00.546 --> 00:44:03.300
this is sort of what
a word vector is.

00:44:04.490 --> 00:44:06.845
These are the words you're
trying to understand

00:44:06.846 --> 00:44:08.155
what they mean, and
then you just say,

00:44:08.156 --> 00:44:10.615
"How often did 'hat' occur within

00:44:10.616 --> 00:44:12.305
some Window of maybe 10 words

00:44:12.306 --> 00:44:14.340
of rock or paper or scholar,

00:44:14.341 --> 00:44:17.220
and how often did
the word obscure."

00:44:17.600 --> 00:44:20.070
Occur within these words
and, of course,

00:44:20.071 --> 00:44:22.580
you do that with two million
other words or something,

00:44:22.581 --> 00:44:24.460
but I can only draw
two dimensions,

00:44:24.461 --> 00:44:26.300
and then you have vectors

00:44:26.301 --> 00:44:28.110
that come from just these counts.

00:44:28.111 --> 00:44:29.230
So, it's a very simple thing.

00:44:29.231 --> 00:44:32.325
It's like a giant Excel
spreadsheet or something.

00:44:32.326 --> 00:44:36.400
Then, you just measure
the cosine between these angles.

00:44:36.401 --> 00:44:37.770
It's semantics.

00:44:37.771 --> 00:44:40.890
That's saying how similar
in meaning are these words.

00:44:40.891 --> 00:44:43.580
How related are they? So,
that's all that's going on

00:44:43.581 --> 00:44:46.555
and this is how
search engines work.

00:44:46.556 --> 00:44:49.205
That's why you can type in
four words and get a page

00:44:49.206 --> 00:44:50.440
and it's not all that's going

00:44:50.441 --> 00:44:52.170
on as you guys probably
know better than me,

00:44:52.171 --> 00:44:54.510
but it's the basic
underlying technology

00:44:54.511 --> 00:44:57.130
that first gets that
working for you.

00:44:59.070 --> 00:45:02.995
For the science paper, we
used two different corpuses.

00:45:02.996 --> 00:45:05.220
We used the Stanford one which is

00:45:05.221 --> 00:45:06.620
