00:48:29.661 --> 00:48:31.705
and what a surprise we
can learn it quickly.

00:48:31.706 --> 00:48:34.090
But, there's
no more general purpose

00:48:34.091 --> 00:48:35.305
learning than Bayes rule.

00:48:35.306 --> 00:48:37.570
I mean, come on you know that so.

00:48:37.571 --> 00:48:40.590
So, yeah, AI's
already superhuman,

00:48:40.591 --> 00:48:44.750
but people aren't
recognizing it as being so,

00:48:44.751 --> 00:48:46.880
and then we can and saw
these kinds of hazards.

00:48:46.881 --> 00:48:48.320
So, like the economic hazard,

00:48:48.321 --> 00:48:49.805
I mentioned this before.

00:48:49.806 --> 00:48:53.250
I just want to talk about
where does this come from.

00:48:53.251 --> 00:48:55.305
Why are we having
this surging inequality.

00:48:55.306 --> 00:48:57.090
I think part of the problem.

00:48:57.091 --> 00:49:00.650
Well this is again this is
we have a paper next slide.

00:49:00.651 --> 00:49:02.400
We have a paper
account about this,

00:49:02.401 --> 00:49:04.885
but we think what's
going on is when you

00:49:04.886 --> 00:49:08.480
introduce technology that
reduces the cost of distance,

00:49:08.481 --> 00:49:10.935
then you're just going
to have fewer winners.

00:49:10.936 --> 00:49:13.170
So, in the late 19th
century that was like

00:49:13.171 --> 00:49:15.830
rail, it was telegraph.

00:49:15.831 --> 00:49:19.360
So, the newspapers were
getting this and oil.

00:49:19.361 --> 00:49:21.495
Oil was lighter and cheaper.

00:49:21.496 --> 00:49:23.790
So, it's not that it eliminated
the cost of distance,

00:49:23.791 --> 00:49:25.440
but massively reduced it.

00:49:25.441 --> 00:49:28.295
So, if, say we're going to
go for beer after this,

00:49:28.296 --> 00:49:30.595
and say we were actually
in a town not on a campus.

00:49:30.596 --> 00:49:33.680
We wouldn't go to the best pub
in the entire town.

00:49:33.681 --> 00:49:35.240
We would go to
some combination of

00:49:35.241 --> 00:49:37.265
a good pub and one
that's near to us.

00:49:37.266 --> 00:49:38.560
So, that's one of
the ways you can

00:49:38.561 --> 00:49:39.590
have lots of pubs and lots of

00:49:39.591 --> 00:49:41.810
people can get money But,

00:49:41.811 --> 00:49:43.620
with all the sudden you can get

00:49:43.621 --> 00:49:46.149
your newspaper across
the entire country

00:49:46.150 --> 00:49:48.000
then there's
fewer newspapers because

00:49:48.001 --> 00:49:49.230
everybody wants to go

00:49:49.231 --> 00:49:51.530
get the New York Times,
or whatever.

00:49:51.531 --> 00:49:53.950
Not my favorite newspaper
and so, whatever.

00:49:53.951 --> 00:49:59.870
So, when the great
corpora happens,

00:49:59.871 --> 00:50:01.660
some people say, "Oh,
there was just a war.

00:50:01.661 --> 00:50:03.655
That's just a war.
It's not just the war.

00:50:03.656 --> 00:50:05.215
As I mentioned before the war,

00:50:05.216 --> 00:50:08.335
it was a massive shift
in spending.

00:50:08.336 --> 00:50:10.040
The war was part of that.

00:50:10.041 --> 00:50:11.100
The war effort was part of that,

00:50:11.101 --> 00:50:13.965
but it continued beyond that
into through the Cold War,

00:50:13.966 --> 00:50:16.210
a shift in Spain that
was redistribution.

00:50:16.211 --> 00:50:17.950
Maybe it was when we were given

00:50:17.951 --> 00:50:19.300
incredibly high amounts of

00:50:19.301 --> 00:50:21.505
wages to uneducated white males.

00:50:21.506 --> 00:50:23.930
But, somehow we were
getting money out into

00:50:23.931 --> 00:50:28.360
the economy and we were
getting less inequality.

00:50:28.500 --> 00:50:34.725
Literally in 1945, I
mean so this is FDR.

00:50:34.726 --> 00:50:39.040
So, after one World War and
the financial crash of '28,

00:50:39.041 --> 00:50:41.990
there was a coalition
between the elite and

00:50:41.991 --> 00:50:45.095
the proletariat in
the Democrat Party in America,

00:50:45.096 --> 00:50:46.340
and that was the New Deal

00:50:46.341 --> 00:50:48.345
and that was
massive redistribution.

00:50:48.346 --> 00:50:50.640
At the end of 1945,

00:50:50.641 --> 00:50:53.115
globally we're sitting around in

00:50:53.116 --> 00:50:55.850
Europe agreeing that
there would no longer be

00:50:55.851 --> 00:50:57.670
wealth extraction and wealth

00:50:57.671 --> 00:50:59.580
and the banking laws by which

00:50:59.581 --> 00:51:02.305
national wealth
extraction occurred

00:51:02.306 --> 00:51:06.475
were eliminated for about 20
years and then we innovated.

00:51:06.476 --> 00:51:09.060
Because they figured out that
the countries in Europe,

00:51:09.061 --> 00:51:10.790
they found into fascism
were the ones that

00:51:10.791 --> 00:51:12.810
had the highest inequality
that the wealth is

00:51:12.811 --> 00:51:14.270
being extracted
because they'd sort of

00:51:14.271 --> 00:51:15.450
arbitrarily been part of

00:51:15.451 --> 00:51:17.720
the set to have lost
the First World War.

00:51:17.721 --> 00:51:20.690
So, we need to get everybody to

00:51:20.691 --> 00:51:25.510
realize that we want to
get more into this area,

00:51:25.511 --> 00:51:28.330
not that the '60s and '70s
were completely smooth either,

00:51:28.331 --> 00:51:30.850
but it wasn't like a World War I.

00:51:32.410 --> 00:51:34.665
Empirically, it turns out that

00:51:34.666 --> 00:51:36.750
Gini coefficient is around 0.7.

00:51:36.751 --> 00:51:39.210
We're not talking about
completely egalitarianism here.

00:51:39.211 --> 00:51:40.640
We're not saying like socialism

00:51:40.641 --> 00:51:41.895
is the same of everything.

00:51:41.896 --> 00:51:43.430
You do want to get more money

00:51:43.431 --> 00:51:45.570
to people that are
doing good work.

00:51:45.571 --> 00:51:52.515
We're just talking about how
steep is the inequality.

00:51:52.516 --> 00:51:54.580
This is actually people
who are got into

00:51:54.581 --> 00:51:56.130
archive within the last week.

00:51:56.131 --> 00:51:57.830
Again, I'm not going to
talk about it too much

00:51:57.831 --> 00:51:59.540
but I was just on

00:51:59.541 --> 00:52:03.725
slide but we started
from the assumption,

00:52:03.726 --> 00:52:05.770
as I mentioned,
the speed question.

00:52:05.771 --> 00:52:10.770
Why is inequality and political
polarization correlated?

00:52:10.771 --> 00:52:12.840
What we assume in the model is

00:52:12.841 --> 00:52:14.675
that in-group cooperation is

00:52:14.676 --> 00:52:19.070
less likely to pay off well
but also less likely to fail.

00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:25.749
What we showed was that if
the economy is getting worse,

00:52:25.750 --> 00:52:28.470
it makes sense after
a certain point

00:52:28.471 --> 00:52:32.420
to give up the
opportunity of having

00:52:32.421 --> 00:52:34.005
a higher expected outcome

00:52:34.006 --> 00:52:35.470
in order to make
sure that you have

00:52:35.471 --> 00:52:37.520
at least enough outcome

00:52:37.521 --> 00:52:39.995
so that you maintain
your solvency.

00:52:39.996 --> 00:52:44.000
The trick here is that you

00:52:44.001 --> 00:52:45.210
can't have a society without

00:52:45.211 --> 00:52:47.100
the individuals being
able to survive.

00:52:47.101 --> 00:52:49.120
So, you can only ask individuals

00:52:49.121 --> 00:52:51.415
to give so much to society.

00:52:51.416 --> 00:52:53.925
However, the interesting
thing is that

00:52:53.926 --> 00:52:56.680
if the economy keeps
getting even worse,

00:52:56.681 --> 00:52:58.455
so that you can't be solvent

00:52:58.456 --> 00:53:00.240
just by doing that
in-group cooperation,

00:53:00.241 --> 00:53:02.600
then you need to actually
desperately do something and

00:53:02.601 --> 00:53:04.140
going to working with

00:53:04.141 --> 00:53:06.630
the out-group works
makes sense again.

00:53:07.300 --> 00:53:12.685
So, that's the suspect down here.

00:53:12.686 --> 00:53:15.050
Then again in some context,

00:53:15.051 --> 00:53:16.660
and I really don't like go into

00:53:16.661 --> 00:53:18.595
this too much before we
get through peer review,

00:53:18.596 --> 00:53:19.810
but we've shown this on a couple

00:53:19.811 --> 00:53:21.925
of models that polarization,

00:53:21.926 --> 00:53:23.930
it's much easier to
get gradual increase

00:53:23.931 --> 00:53:26.070
than to get gradual decrease.

00:53:26.071 --> 00:53:29.065
Again because that non-linearity
at that one point.

00:53:29.066 --> 00:53:31.290
So, that might explain
why you tend to

00:53:31.291 --> 00:53:35.025
see these big corrections
like the new deal.

00:53:35.026 --> 00:53:37.500
Rather than like everything

00:53:37.501 --> 00:53:39.995
to start slowly, relaxing again.

